.TH GPU-ARBITER-CONFIG 5 "June 2026" "gpu-arbiter" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
gpu\-arbiter\-config \- configuration file for gpu\-arbiter(8)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/gpu-arbiter/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR gpu-arbiter (8)
reads its configuration from a TOML file (by default
.IR /etc/gpu-arbiter/config.toml ,
typically rendered by a configuration\-management tool). A
.B missing file is not an error
\(em it yields the built\-in defaults, so the daemon is fully usable with zero
configuration. Every key is optional; a sparse file overrides only the keys it
names and leaves the rest at their defaults.
.PP
An
.B unknown or misspelled key
at any level (top\-level, a
.B [[managed_units]]
entry, or a
.B [[game_patterns]]
entry) is a
.B parse error
naming the offending key, not a silently\-ignored no\-op \(em run
.B gpu\-arbiter \-\-check\-config
after editing this file.
.SH TOP-LEVEL KEYS
.TP
.B enabled
Boolean. Default
.BR true .
Master enable. When
.BR false ,
the daemon logs that it is disabled and exits cleanly (a quick disable without
touching systemd).
.TP
.B port
Integer (u16). Default
.BR 48750 .
HTTP listen port for the read\-only surface, bound to
.BR bind .
.TP
.B bind
String (IP address). Default
.BR """127.0.0.1""" .
TCP bind address for the HTTP control surface. Accepts any IPv4 or IPv6
address literal; a malformed value is a parse error naming the bad string.
Loopback\-only by default; set this to a LAN address to let other hosts read
.IR /status " and " /metrics .
On
.B Windows
this also bounds the write routes, which are gated to loopback peers only
regardless of
.BR bind ,
so widening it there only exposes the read\-only surface. On
.B Linux
firewall the port yourself if you widen it.
.TP
.B socket_path
String (filesystem path). Default
.BR """/run/gpu-arbiter/gpu-arbiter.sock""" .
Path to the unix control socket serving the write path
.RB ( "POST /units/{unit}/start|stop" )
on
.BR Linux ,
created mode 0600, root\-owned \(em local root only, no bearer tokens. The
parent directory is created if missing at mode 0700, root\-owned, and is
itself part of the auth boundary, not just the socket file's own mode: it
closes the brief window between the socket file being created (bind) and its
mode being pinned to 0600 (chmod), during which another local user could
otherwise attempt a connection. Before binding, an existing socket file is
probed with a short connect: if a live process answers (another
gpu\-arbiter instance), startup fails rather than stealing its socket; only
a genuinely stale file from an unclean prior shutdown (nothing listening) is
removed. A failure to bind either listener (this socket or the TCP
.BR port )
is fatal at startup \(em the daemon exits nonzero so the service manager
surfaces and retries it, instead of half\-running without a control surface.
An
.B empty string
disables the unix socket entirely, leaving Linux with no write path at all.
Ignored on
.BR Windows ,
where the write routes are served on the TCP port instead \(em see
.B bind
above.
.TP
.B managed_units
Array of tables. Default: a single entry \(em
.BR "unit = ""ollama.service""" ,
.BR "eager_restart = true" ,
.BR "vram_match = ""ollama""" ,
.BR "kind = ""ollama""" .
The ordered list of systemd units the arbiter evicts from the GPU on a game
launch and restores when gaming ends; eviction runs in array order. An
explicit empty array disables eviction entirely. See
.B MANAGED UNIT FIELDS
below.
.TP
.B eviction_timeout_s
Integer (seconds). Default
.BR 5 .
How long to wait for a graceful teardown (VRAM drop below
.BR vram_free_threshold_mb )
before escalating to SIGKILL.
.TP
.B yield_timeout_s
Integer (seconds). Default
.BR 3 .
Default budget for a
.B cooperative
release, used by units that set
.B yield_cmd
but no per\-unit
.BR yield_timeout_s .
Deliberately shorter than
.BR eviction_timeout_s :
this is the
.I first
of two eviction stages and its budget is spent before the stop path even
begins, so a generous value here directly delays a game getting the GPU. Tune
it from the
.B gpu_arbiter_eviction_duration_seconds{stage="yield"}
histogram rather than by guessing. See
.B THE PRIORITY LADDER
below.
.TP
.B game_priority
Integer 0\(en255. Default
.BR 100 .
The priority a detected
.B game
claims at; higher wins. The default sits above every unit's default
.B priority
.RB ( 50 ),
which is what makes gaming preempt everything. Lowering it below a tenant's
priority would let that tenant survive a game launch \(em a real option (a
tenant you never want interrupted), but a deliberate one.
.TP
.B vram_free_threshold_mb
Integer (MiB). Default
.BR 2000 .
VRAM\-used strictly below this value means the GPU (or, when attribution is
available, the evicting unit itself) is considered "freed" after eviction.
The graceful\-eviction poll gates on the evicting unit's
.B own
attributed VRAM (cgroup, then
.BR vram_match ,
same precedence as
.I /status
attribution) when available, falling back to total GPU VRAM otherwise \(em
see
.B vram_match
below.
.TP
.B reconcile_interval_s
Integer (seconds). Default
.BR 30 .
Slow backstop reconcile interval. Detection itself is event\-driven (cn_proc);
this only covers dropped events.
.TP
.B detect_steam
Boolean. Default
.BR true .
Match
.I "SteamLaunch AppId="
in exec'd cmdlines (covers all Steam games via a
.I steam:<appid>
claim).
.TP
.B game_patterns
Array of tables. Default empty. Cmdline\-substring patterns for non\-Steam
launchers. Each entry has:
.RS
.TP
.B name
String. Human\-readable claim name (becomes
.IR pattern:<name> ).
.TP
.B match
String. Substring matched against a process cmdline.
.RE
.TP
.B gpu_backend
String. Default
.BR """auto""" .
Selects the GPU backend used to read VRAM and (on NVIDIA) per\-process compute
usage. One of
.BR """auto""" ,
.BR """nvidia""" ,
or
.BR """amd""" .
.B auto
probes for
.B nvidia-smi
on
.I PATH
and uses the NVIDIA backend if present, otherwise falls back to the AMD
.I amdgpu
sysfs backend
.RI ( /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_* ),
and finally to NVIDIA if neither can be detected.
.B nvidia
and
.B amd
force the respective backend. On AMD there is no per\-process VRAM via sysfs, so
per\-unit
.I /status
VRAM attribution degrades to empty (never an error); eviction itself is identical.
.TP
.B presence_detection
Boolean. Default
.BR true .
Watch
.B physical
human\-input devices (keyboard/mouse/gamepad) to report local at\-desk presence in
.I /status
and
.IR /metrics .
Virtual devices injected by Moonlight/Sunshine streaming are excluded by sysfs
parentage. When
.BR false ,
presence is not tracked and reported as unknown.
.TP
.B presence_idle_threshold_s
Integer (seconds). Default
.BR 600 .
Physical\-input silence after which local presence is reported as absent
.RB ( local_present " = 0)."
.SH MANAGED UNIT FIELDS
Each
.B [[managed_units]]
table accepts:
.TP
.B unit
String,
.BR required .
By default the systemd unit the daemon exclusively owns (kept
.I disabled
so systemd never races the daemon). When the
.B *_cmd
overrides below are set, this is instead a
.B free\-form label
for the tenant \(em not necessarily a systemd unit \(em used in
.I /status
and log lines.
.TP
.B eager_restart
Boolean. Default
.BR true .
Restart this unit when gaming ends (eager warm\-up).
.TP
.B priority
Integer 0\(en255. Default
.BR 50 .
This tenant's tier on the priority ladder. A demand at priority
.I P
preempts every unit with
.IR "priority < P" ,
and leaves everything at
.I ">= P"
alone. The comparison is strict, so a unit can never preempt itself and two
units at the same tier coexist rather than fighting. The mid\-range default
means a config that never mentions priorities keeps every unit on one equal
tier \(em pre\-ladder behavior, with a game still evicting them all. See
.B THE PRIORITY LADDER
below.
.TP
.B busy_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none.
Probe for "this tenant has work right now" \(em
.B exit 0 means busy.
This is what lets a tenant
.I preempt
lower tiers rather than merely surviving them; without it a unit is a
preemption target only, never a source (the right default, since a
merely\-running server holding an idle model should not evict anything). Run on
.B every
reconcile pass, so it must be cheap and must not block. A probe that fails to
spawn, times out, or exits non\-zero reads as
.BR "not busy" ,
the conservative direction \(em a broken probe must not be able to evict a
lower tier on a false pretext.
.TP
.B yield_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none.
.B Cooperative
GPU release, tried before any stop: asks the tenant to let go of the GPU while
staying alive. Exit 0 means "the request was accepted",
.B not
"the GPU is free" \(em the daemon then polls
.B busy_cmd
until the tenant reports not busy. Failing to release within
.B yield_timeout_s
escalates to the ordinary stop \(-> wait \(-> kill path, so a tenant that
ignores or mishandles the request can never hold the GPU against a higher tier.
.IP
.B "Ignored unless busy_cmd is also set."
Release would be unobservable, so the daemon logs a warning, skips the yield
stage entirely, and stops the unit instead. It escalates immediately rather
than sleeping out the budget: waiting cannot produce information it is
structurally unable to observe.
.TP
.B resume_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none. Undo for
.BR yield_cmd ,
letting the tenant use the GPU again. Run on the restore path
.B before
any start, and expected to be
.BR idempotent :
the daemon deliberately does not track whether a given unit was yielded or
stopped, because that state would have to survive a daemon restart to be
trustworthy. Running an idempotent resume unconditionally is cheaper and
cannot desync.
.TP
.B yield_timeout_s
Integer (seconds). Default: none (falls back to the top\-level
.BR yield_timeout_s ).
How long to wait for this unit's cooperative release before escalating to the
stop path. Per\-unit because the right value is tenant\-specific: parking a
17 GB model to host RAM is not the same operation as draining a small one.
.TP
.B stop_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none. Override for stopping/evicting the
tenant. When unset, the daemon runs
.BR "systemctl stop <unit>" .
.TP
.B start_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none. Override for starting the tenant.
When unset, the daemon runs
.BR "systemctl start <unit>" .
.TP
.B is_active_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none. Override for the liveness check; its
.B exit 0 means running.
When unset, the daemon runs
.BR "systemctl is-active <unit>" .
.TP
.B kill_cmd
String or array of strings. Default: none. Override for the SIGKILL escalation
step. When unset off systemd it re\-runs
.B stop_cmd
as a best\-effort second teardown; under systemd the escalation is
.BR "systemctl kill -s SIGKILL <unit>" .
.PP
The
.B *_cmd
overrides let the daemon drive a tenant on a host without systemd (OpenRC,
runit, or plain processes). Like
.BR introspect_cmd ,
each command is
.B shell\-free
\(em an explicit argv split on whitespace and spawned directly (never
.BR "sh -c" ),
so a unit name or path cannot inject. An argument that contains a space cannot
be represented. When
.B all
.B *_cmd
are unset for a unit it is systemd\-driven exactly as before.
.TP
.B vram_match
String. Default: none.
.B Fallback
substring matched case\-insensitively against
.B nvidia-smi
compute\-process names to attribute this unit's VRAM in
.IR /status .
For a
.B systemd\-supervised
unit, cgroup PID resolution attributes VRAM automatically with
.B no config needed
\(em it reads
.I /proc/<pid>/cgroup
to find the owning unit directly, so it cannot be fooled by a wrapper binary
(a venv interpreter, a launcher script) the way this name\-substring match
can be.
.B vram_match
is consulted whenever cgroup resolution does not produce a match for a given
poll. For command\-driven
.RB ( *_cmd )
units and non\-systemd hosts, no cgroup path ever resolves to a configured
unit name, so it is the
.B only
attribution channel; for a systemd\-supervised unit it can also fire on an
occasional poll where cgroup resolution has not caught up yet. When unset
(and no cgroup match applies), no VRAM figure is reported for the unit (the
field is omitted, not reported as 0).
.TP
.B kind
String. Default: none. Introspection backend selector for the
.I /status
.B models[]
list. The only recognized value is
.BR """ollama""" ,
which runs
.B "ollama ps"
and parses its table for loaded model names. Any other non\-empty value reports
.B no
models and
.B suppresses
the name\-based heuristic below: an explicit non\-Ollama
.B kind
is taken as "this tenant is not Ollama", so e.g.\&
.B "kind = ""vllm"""
on a unit named
.I ollama.service
will
.B not
run
.BR "ollama ps" .
The name\-based heuristic applies
.B only
when
.B kind
is unset
.B and
.B introspect_cmd
is unset: a
.B unit
name containing
.I ollama
(case\-insensitive) is then treated as
.BR """ollama""" .
.B kind
is bypassed entirely when
.B introspect_cmd
is non\-blank \(em the precedence short\-circuits at
.B introspect_cmd
and never evaluates
.BR kind .
.TP
.B introspect_cmd
String. Default: none. An explicit command whose standard output lists the
unit's loaded model/process names \(em
.B one name per line,
each trimmed, with empty lines dropped. The string is split on whitespace into
an
.B argv
and run
.B shell\-free
(no shell, no quoting, no globbing, no variable expansion): the first token is
the program, the rest are arguments.
.B Warning:
because parsing is plain whitespace splitting with no quoting, an argument that
.B contains a space cannot be represented
\(em a path such as
.I "/path/with spaces/file"
is split into the two separate tokens
.I /path/with
and
.I spaces/file
and the command will fail. Do not use embedded spaces in the program path or any
argument.
.PP
When set to a non\-blank value within the length limit, it
.B takes precedence
over
.B kind
and the name heuristic. A
.B blank, whitespace\-only, or over\-length
value (longer than
.B 1024
bytes) is treated as
.B unset
and falls through to the next precedence level
.RB ( kind ,
then the name heuristic). The 1024\-byte cap is a footgun guard against an
operator typo overrunning the OS argv limit; a real command is far shorter.
Best\-effort: a missing binary, a non\-zero exit, an empty argv, or a query timeout
yields no models (never an error). The command is
.B bounded to 2 seconds
(the
.I INTROSPECTION_TIMEOUT ,
tighter than the 10\-second bound on
.BR stop_cmd / start_cmd / is_active_cmd / kill_cmd ,
since this query runs on every reconcile pass's
.I /status
refresh and must stay fast); a custom introspection command that runs longer
is killed and silently yields an empty list, so keep it fast.
.SH THE PRIORITY LADDER
Gaming\-beats\-everything is the floor, not the whole model. Tenants also rank
against
.I each other,
and a tenant that supports it can be asked to let go of the GPU without being
killed.
.PP
Every unit has a
.B priority
(default 50); a game claims at
.B game_priority
(default 100). A demand at priority
.I P
preempts every unit with
.I "priority < P."
Because the comparison is strict, equal tiers coexist, and a config that never
mentions priorities behaves exactly as it did before the ladder existed.
.PP
A unit only
.I preempts
a lower tier when its
.B busy_cmd
exits 0. Inter\-tenant preemption deliberately does
.B not
move
.I state
to
.I gaming
or
.IR evicting :
those words are the
.I /status
contract for "a game owns the GPU, back off entirely", and reporting them
because one tenant outranked another would tell a remote AI\-routing consumer
the box is unavailable for AI work at exactly the moment it is doing AI work.
Preemption is visible through
.I units[].running
instead.
.PP
When a unit sets
.BR yield_cmd ,
eviction runs in two stages rather than one:
.IP 1. 4
.B Yield
\(em run
.B yield_cmd
and poll
.B busy_cmd
until the tenant reports not busy, up to
.BR yield_timeout_s .
The tenant stays alive; it just parks its model (for a PyTorch service,
typically
.B model.cpu()
+
.BR torch.cuda.empty_cache() ).
On success the unit is never stopped \(em no in\-flight work is lost and there
is no cold model reload afterwards.
.IP 2. 4
.B Stop
\(em the ordinary stop \(-> poll\-VRAM \(-> SIGKILL path, reached whenever the
yield times out, exits non\-zero, or is not configured.
.PP
Tune both budgets from the
.B gpu_arbiter_eviction_duration_seconds{stage=...}
histogram; the yield/stop split exists precisely to show whether the
cooperative stage is paying for itself or merely adding latency ahead of a stop
that was always going to happen.
.SH INTROSPECTION RESOLUTION
For each managed unit, the
.B models[]
backend is resolved purely, taking the
.B first
matching rule in this order:
.IP 1. 4
.B introspect_cmd
is set and non\-blank \(rA run that argv, one name per stdout line.
.RB ( A
blank/whitespace\-only value does
.B not
match here; it is treated as unset and resolution continues.)
.IP 2. 4
else
.B kind
==
.B """ollama"""
\(rA run
.BR "ollama ps" .
.IP 3. 4
else
.B kind
is set to any
.B other
(non\-"ollama") value \(rA report
.B no
models. An explicit non\-Ollama
.B kind
.B suppresses
the name heuristic in step 4, even for a unit named
.IR ollama.service .
.IP 4. 4
else
.RB ( kind
unset
.B and
.B introspect_cmd
unset) \(rA name heuristic: if the
.B unit
name contains
.I ollama
(case\-insensitive), run
.BR "ollama ps" ;
otherwise report no models.
.PP
An Ollama\-kinded or Ollama\-named unit therefore introspects via
.B "ollama ps"
by default (steps 2 and 4), any other tenant can report its own model/process
list via
.B introspect_cmd
(step 1), and an explicit non\-Ollama
.B kind
opts a unit out of Ollama introspection entirely (step 3).
.SH EXAMPLES
A two\-tenant config where Ollama and a vLLM server both yield to gaming, the
vLLM server reporting its own loaded\-model list via an explicit command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
port = 48750

[[managed_units]]
unit = "ollama.service"
eager_restart = true
vram_match = "ollama"
kind = "ollama"

[[managed_units]]
unit = "vllm.service"
eager_restart = true
vram_match = "vllm"
introspect_cmd = "vllm-cli list-models"

[[game_patterns]]
name = "heroic"
match = "Heroic"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpu-arbiter (8)
